In the quest of middle age, I've been [emulating Mac OS 9](/posts/20260810-newsheep-sheepshaver-mac-os-9-2-apple-silicon-jit/) and [getting Johnny Castaway running in a browser](/posts/20261019-johnny-castaway-browser/). Playing board games with my kids has me revisiting more of that stuff too. I have a rant brewing about the quality of *The Game of Life* these days, but that can wait.

The '90s seem to be back in style, though much of what I notice is the grungier side: distressed type and rough textures. I keep going back to the chaotic graphics on Japanese Sonic covers and TurboGrafx-16 ads. I always thought these were impressive. On the Sonic covers, enormous lettering, bright colours, squiggles and zigzag edges all fit around a character who already takes up most of the cover.

I wanted some of that personality here. I'd only recently redesigned the site, and the analytical style suited my work, but it felt too clinical for my own blog.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - Japanese Sonic covers supplied the yellow disc, bold colour fields, compressed type and patterned edges.
> - A TurboGrafx-16 ad suggested a cut-paper treatment for selected images.
> - The homepage gets the expressive composition; the articles keep a clear reading column and upright text.

## From a datasheet to a cover

My [redesign this summer](/posts/20260808-making-information-actionable-for-24-years/) brought a heavy data angle: graph paper, numbered sections, small labels and rules around most things. By the time I'd added them all, the homepage looked like a technical document.

