### How the collage works

`kh-collage` is ordinary HTML and CSS. A Nunjucks macro puts the complete image in front of three decorative copies, taken from either that image or a separate detail shot. CSS positions those copies around it and cuts their edges with `clip-path`. One static fragment looks like this:

```css
.kh-collage__fragment--a {
  clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 22%, 76% 100%);
  transform: rotate(-12deg);
}
```

The optional parallax is a small script that maps the pointer position to two CSS custom properties, `--kh-collage-x` and `--kh-collage-y`. Each layer multiplies those values by a different distance in its transform, so the pieces move different amounts. Updates use `requestAnimationFrame`. Nothing is being redrawn into a canvas or generated as you move the pointer.

The caption stays still. Decorative copies have empty alt text and are hidden from assistive technology. Touch devices and reduced-motion settings get the static composition; switching CSS off leaves the main image and caption. The [component reference includes the macro call and implementation details](/style-guide/components/#cut-paper-collage).

## Let the article settle down

I kept most of the decoration around the edges of the [article layout](/posts/20261024-when-one-person-leaves-a-team-leaves/). The title gets the yellow disc and condensed type; the paragraphs sit on near-white paper in Plex Sans. The contents list is a native HTML disclosure, so opening it doesn't need a script.

[Anton](https://github.com/google/fonts/tree/main/ofl/anton) handles the other display headings, Plex Sans the paragraphs and controls, and [League Mono](https://github.com/theleagueof/league-mono) the code and small labels. The fonts are self-hosted.

The circle and diagonals are CSS shapes. SVG assets supply the margin marks and serrated edges. Most of the presentation works without JavaScript; the optional collage parallax is a small enhancement.

## A site I recognise

The analytical bits of the site are still there. I just wanted it to look a bit less corporate and a bit more like somewhere I'd choose to spend time.

CSS is still optional. The [CSS toggle I wrote about last year](/posts/20250906-css-naked-css-only/) removes the styling, leaving native HTML. The text remains readable, links work and the contents list still opens.

### Artwork and further references

- **Sonic packaging:** [Yoichi Takahashi's account of the original packages](https://game.watch.impress.co.jp/docs/news/1279221.html), including his commission of Akira Watanabe's character illustrations. [Hayato Takebayashi's own post](https://x.com/pantaloon_n/status/1320632944696852482) confirms his *Spinball* packaging credit. [VGDensetsu](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/) records the covers and detailed illustration credits. Artwork and branding © SEGA.
- **TurboGrafx-16 print ad:** [*Electronic Gaming Monthly* #2, July/August 1989, via TurboPlay Magazine Archives](https://archives.tg-16.com/EGM_1989_07.htm). Individual design and art-direction credits remain unconfirmed. [*Television Digest*, 2 October 1989, p. 13](https://www.worldradiohistory.com/Archive-TV-Digest/80s/1989/Television-Digest-1989-10.pdf#page=13), names DDB Needham as the campaign agency that October; it does not credit this spread.
- **A later graphic comparison:** [SEGA's *Sonic Origins Plus* Japanese cover](https://origins.sonicthehedgehog.com/posters/index.html) returns to the yellow disc, tilted labels, geometric marks and serrated edge.

